The code statements that lead to the bug are:
} else {
    skb_add_rx_frag(skb, skb_shinfo(skb)->nr_frags,
                    page, 0, req->actual_length,
                    PAGE_SIZE);
    page = NULL;
}
Reason:
This else branch is executed when a previous fragment already exists in the skb. It unconditionally adds a new fragment via `skb_add_rx_frag()` without checking whether the number of fragments (`skb_shinfo(skb)->nr_frags`) has reached `MAX_SKB_FRAGS`. A malicious USB device can send an unbounded sequence of full-page bulk transfers, causing the `skb_shared_info->frags[]` array to overflow, leading to memory corruption.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
} else if (skb_shinfo(skb)->nr_frags < MAX_SKB_FRAGS) {
    skb_add_rx_frag(skb, skb_shinfo(skb)->nr_frags,
                    page, 0, req->actual_length,
                    PAGE_SIZE);
    page = NULL;
} else {
    dev_kfree_skb_any(skb);
    pnd->rx_skb = NULL;
    skb = NULL;
    dev->stats.rx_length_errors++;
}
Reason:
The fix adds a condition `skb_shinfo(skb)->nr_frags < MAX_SKB_FRAGS` before adding a new fragment. If the fragment count is below the limit, the fragment is added normally. If the limit is reached, the skb is freed, the pointer `pnd->rx_skb` is reset to `NULL`, and the `rx_length_errors` statistic is incremented, effectively dropping the packet and preventing any out-of-bounds write to the `frags[]` array. This matches the pattern used in other drivers (e.g., t7xx).

